%!testlib
% How many composites in GLOBAL virtual memory a program can reach and write,
% and a digest of which ones.
%
% A restore does not reach global virtual memory (PLRM 3.7.2), so a write into
% one of these stands for the rest of the job; and the machinery's namespaces
% are reachable, because bind freezes the dictionary a helper names into the
% helper's body and a program can read that body.
%
% ONLY THE TWO NUMBERS ARE PRINTED. The paths are not, and are not written
% down anywhere the tree is published from: a list of the objects that still
% have the property is a map for whoever wants to use them, and this test runs
% in public logs. The digest is enough to hold the set: it changes if the set
% changes, and the count says which way. A developer who needs to see the
% objects runs the sweep locally, where nothing is published.
%
% The sweep is .vmsweep, an interpreter assist. It has to be: an object's
% access belongs to the reference and not to the value for every composite but
% the dictionary (PLRM 3.3.2), so a walk written in PostScript is refused by
% every container that is already read-only. Being dot-named it is moved into
% .internaldict at the lockdown, so a program cannot call it.

/ID 1183615869 internaldict def
/BANK 1 def
/KIND 0 def

% The walk takes its own roots from the context, so there are none to pass
% and none kept anywhere a program could read. What is passed is the
% exemption, the bank, and which question to ask.
/sweep { BANK KIND ID /.vmsweep get exec } bind def

% An order-independent digest: each path is folded to a number and the numbers
% are summed, so the order the walk happened to take does not enter into it.
% The modulus keeps every intermediate inside the integer range, which is what
% makes the answer the same whatever width the build is.
/M 65521 def
/fold {                      % string . int
  /s exch def /h 1 def
  0 1 s length 1 sub { s exch get h 257 mul add M mod /h exch def } for
  h
} bind def

/total 0 def /sum 0 def
/tally {                     % -  .  -
  sweep
  dup length /n exch def
  /total total n add def
  0 1 n 1 sub { 1 index exch get fold sum add M mod /sum exch def } for
  pop
} bind def

tally

(REACHABLE-WRITABLE-GLOBAL ) print total =print ( ) print sum ==
(DECLARED-WRITABLE-STORAGE ) print ID /.vmdeclaredcount get exec ==

% The same question of local virtual memory. A write there is undone by a
% restore and by the job boundary, so what it buys a program is the rest of
% the job -- which, for a workload that is one job per request, is the whole
% of the request. The two are reported apart because what they guarantee is
% not the same, and a single number would hide the stronger of the two.
% Rooted at the machinery's own namespaces and NOT at systemdict, which
% reaches userdict and so reaches whatever the running program has defined --
% a number that moved when this file was edited would hold nothing. Those
% three are machinery only: a program can no longer store into them.
/total 0 def /sum 0 def /BANK 2 def
tally
(REACHABLE-WRITABLE-LOCAL ) print total =print ( ) print sum ==

% The constants bind froze into the machinery's bodies. They hold no
% executable array, so the sweep above passes over them, and they are what
% the machinery reads to decide how to read what a program gave it -- how
% many components a space costs, which bit depths an image may declare.
% A program that reads a body reaches one and may write it. Counted here so
% that each is either sealed where it is defined or answered for; the count
% is a ratchet like the others.
/total 0 def /sum 0 def /BANK 1 def /KIND 1 def
tally
(FROZEN-WRITABLE-CONSTANTS ) print total =print ( ) print sum ==

% The other half of that question: what a body reaches that the job store
% holds. The store is where the machinery keeps what it writes as it runs,
% so the rule above passes over it -- a reference baked into a body reaches
% state, and state reached through code is still state. That is the one
% place the constant rule gives way, so its size is counted here rather
% than left to be discovered. The two counts partition what a body reaches.
/total 0 def /sum 0 def /BANK 1 def /KIND 2 def
tally
(STORE-SHIELDED-STATE ) print total =print ( ) print sum ==

% And what execute-only has closed: a constant a body froze in that no route
% reaches through readable containers. A program can make the machinery run
% past it and cannot take it, so it is not the hazard the count above is --
% but it is counted, because a census that stopped seeing behind an
% execute-only body would report a hazard closed and a question abandoned
% with the same number.
/total 0 def /sum 0 def /BANK 1 def /KIND 5 def
tally
(BODY-CLOSED-CONSTANTS ) print total =print ( ) print sum ==

% And the question the local pair above cannot ask. Its dictionary rule
% ends in "and global", because sealing the local tables stops the
% interpreter working -- so `local 0 0` can only ever mean "no executable
% array in local virtual memory". A table in LOCAL memory that a program
% can write and the machinery can be made to run out of is counted here
% instead, where the number cannot be mistaken for the stronger claim.
/total 0 def /sum 0 def /BANK 2 def /KIND 6 def
tally
(LOCAL-CODE-TABLES ) print total =print ( ) print sum ==
% What closing the bodies is for. A body is what the machinery RUNS, and
% until it is closed to reading it is also something a program can read:
% bind freezes the dictionary a helper names into the helper's body, so a
% body a program can read is a namespace a program can reach. Execute-only
% (PLRM 3.3.2) leaves the machinery able to run it and takes the reading
% away, and this counts what is left -- a body the walk reaches through
% readable containers that a program can still read.
%
% It is asked of both banks, because a body is closed in both. A ratchet
% like the others: it may fall and it may not rise.
/total 0 def /sum 0 def /BANK 3 def /KIND 7 def
tally
(READABLE-BODIES ) print total =print ( ) print sum ==

% What the INTERPRETER reaches that the walk above does not. Every number
% before this one is about the graph five named roots reach; this one says
% how much of the arena that is. It is asked for here and taken at the
% interpreter's safe point, because it marks from the collector's roots --
% the stacks, the save stack, the name stack -- and marking from inside an
% operator reads a root set that operator's own C frames are holding
% storage out of.
%
% Reported apart from the strings, which are the text of interned names:
% the name tree holds those and no object graph reaches them, so they say
% nothing about coverage and would move whenever a name was interned.
ID /.vmblindcount get exec
pop                 % the name strings drop
exch                % containers under, reached on top
(WALK-BLIND-REACHED ) print ==

(WALK-BLIND-CONTAINERS ) print ==

(SWEPT\n) print
flush
quit
